    thesis:'A creator can build institutional capacity before forming a company—method, evidence, relationships, audience routes, promises, and an archive—but should never confuse that capacity with legal personality, liability protection, tax status, or governance.',
    intro:['The word “house” can describe a cultural practice long before it describes a particular legal entity. A person may already have recurring clients, collaborators, methods, files, samples, domains, social accounts, invoices, licenses, obligations, and a public promise. Those elements form an institution in the editorial sense because they coordinate action and memory. They do not automatically create a corporation, limited-liability shield, employer system, or transferable bundle of rights.','The Small Business Administration’s current guidance distinguishes business structures and explains that structure affects taxes, liability, paperwork, and fundraising. Its registration guidance also makes the requirements contingent on structure, activity, and location. These sources do not select an entity for a fashion creator. They establish why the editorial and legal thresholds must remain separate—and why early institutional discipline makes later professional advice more useful.'],
    movements:[
      ['01 · PRACTICE','What already repeats?','Document the offer, client or audience promise, research method, design sequence, collaborator roles, quality threshold, payment path, correction route, and archive habit. Repetition reveals the institution already operating around the person.','INFERENCE',['S94','S95']],
      ['02 · RECORD','Can the practice prove what happened?','Use written scopes, invoices, contributor credits, versioned files, rights and permissions records, contact consent, expense evidence, tax records, and object identifiers appropriate to the actual jurisdiction and activity.','PROPOSAL',['S39','S95']],
      ['03 · SEPARATE','Which formal questions cannot be solved by good habits?','Entity formation, liability, tax treatment, employment, insurance, securities, licenses, registrations, beneficial ownership, contracting authority, and cross-border activity require fact-specific professional review. A beautiful archive does not create a liability shield.','VERIFIED',['S94','S95']],
      ['04 · TRANSITION','What should move when structure arrives?','Inventory names, domains, accounts, agreements, equipment, files, marks, designs, licenses, debts, receivables, privacy obligations, and archive custody. Counsel and advisers must determine what can be assigned, licensed, contributed, retained, or newly contracted.','PROPOSAL',['S94','S95','S49']]
    ],
    tension:'Celebrating “institution before company” can romanticize informal work, delay protections, conceal unpaid labor, or expose the creator and collaborators to avoidable risk. The point is not to postpone advice. It is to arrive with a legible practice map—and to formalize when the facts, jurisdiction, liability, tax, labor, funding, and continuity needs require it.',
    fieldwork:['Map every recurring promise, role, payment, file, account, right, obligation, and correction route already in use.','Mark the person or entity currently named on each agreement, invoice, domain, account, registration, and insurance record.','List every place where collaborators, customers, money, manufacturing, data, travel, or liability create a professional-review trigger.','Take the transition inventory to qualified legal, tax, insurance, employment, and financial advisers in the relevant jurisdictions.'],
    objectId:'81112',objectUse:'This House of Worth gown survives under a house-level maker attribution, but the object record cannot tell us which entity structure, contracts, or liabilities operated at every moment. Cultural continuity and legal form require separate evidence.',
